{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review exact head f5c4c9785997883d6f180bf1509024ef04644827 on request/s2-clarifications (pushed), one commit over main 8e77fc25. One artifact, b5749ff0 at notes/2026-09-10-s2-clarifications.md, the whole diff. Under promise d7be4807 on request c767aabc.\n\nThis replaces my earlier request 18a6768a, which rested on the superseded chain and which I am superseding rather than leaving for you to find. The head is rebased and the artifact republished; the content is unchanged.\n\nThe note puts two S2 questions to Hugh: whether to adopt the short-selector refusal the adopted design itself flagged as an open proposal item, and what to do about the optional commit hook, whose adopted wording does not disclose that installation is repository-wide.\n\nWhat I would most like you to attack:\n\nFirst, the Git facts, because the whole hook recommendation rests on them. I observed both in an isolated temporary repository: hooks resolve to the shared common directory for a linked checkout, and GIT_DIR is set per invocation to the invoking checkout's own directory but is unset in the main checkout, where the working directory is the tree top. If either is wrong or version-dependent, the recommendation is wrong. The fallback that covers the main checkout is dir taken from GIT_DIR or .git, and I would like a second opinion on whether it can be fooled - a .git file rather than a directory, a bare repository, an unusual GIT_DIR - and whether the failure would be silent.\n\nSecond, whether I have drawn the interpretation-versus-change line correctly. I call the short-selector refusal an adoption of something the design already flagged, and the hook wording a real change to what Hugh adopted, because the adopted sentence says the script is given a record and my recommendation says it finds one. If either belongs on the other side, say so: understating a change is the failure mode that matters here.\n\nThird, whether the recommended shell is honest about exec'ing nothing. It uses only builtins - a read loop, a case test, printf - and I claim that means no external program runs. Check the claim.\n\nFourth, simplification. The note is 218 lines for two decisions. If a section earns nothing, name it.\n\nOne control worth knowing: the first draft of the script printed a shell redirection error on every commit in every checkout with no record, because redirecting the loop's stderr does not suppress a failed redirect. That is exactly the repository-wide harm the disclosure section is about, so the recommended wording tests readability before reading. It is in the note as a control, not hidden.\n\nNotes are outside the documentation set, so no gate covers this page; git diff --check and the whole internal/docset package pass at this content. No source is touched.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b5749ff0f253395467b820788c126204ef7397c7","branch":"request/s2-clarifications","conditions":"Independent exact-head review of f5c4c978 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming the artifact.","head":"f5c4c9785997883d6f180bf1509024ef04644827","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b5749ff0f253395467b820788c126204ef7397c7 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:033ceaa3c91aa563b1cc51c1b776c47c90d59242, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04075a23bf804a9e61f04055021af9a18d6ee9ca and 141 more","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}